    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a really hard time with my signals class. i get the formulas for the fourier transform but i dont really get the actual intuition. like how can one single wave just be made up of a bunch of other waves? can u explain it in a way that actually makes sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine you're in a forest, and you hear the sound of a single bell ringing. At first glance, it seems like just one sound, right? But if you listen closely, you might notice that the sound isn't just one clear, pure tone. It's a mix of many smaller, overlapping sounds. That's kind of like what the Fourier transform does with waves. It takes a complex, jumbled-up wave and breaks it down into simpler, individual waves that, when combined, recreate the original sound. It's like finding out that the bell's sound is actually a symphony of smaller, simpler notes played together. So, even though it might seem strange at first, it's all about understanding the different parts that make up the whole. And just like in a forest, where you might not see the whole picture until you step back, the Fourier transform helps you see the bigger picture of a wave by breaking it down into its smaller parts.",
